  "worked_solution": "The rotation matrix ${R}_{\\theta }$ represents a rotation of angle $\\theta $ counterclockwise about the origin and is given by\n\n$$\n{R}_{\\theta }=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta  & -\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta  & \\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta \\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nComparing $A$ with the rotation matrix, we have \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta >0$ and $\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta <0,$  we must have that $\\theta $ lies in $2$nd quadrant. \n\nDividing the first equation by the second gives\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}\u2061\\theta =-1.\n$$\n\nThe principal value of ${\\theta }_{1}$ is\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{1}=\\operatorname{arctan}\u2061(-1)=-\\frac{\\pi }{4},\n$$\n\nbut this is outside the range $\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le x\\le \\pi .$ To find a value in the required range, we add $\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\theta ={\\theta }_{1}+\\pi =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our matrix represents a rotation of $\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ radians counterclockwise about the origin.\n\nFinally, to find $Av$, we need to rotate $v$ counterclockwise by an angle of $\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ radians, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055662/assets/49ec5421325f8ae5492743b988b1c0d727b2eac9e56f169ad5d55753bae5d69a.png)",
